On Jan 3, 1:48 pm, Matthias Sommer <sunny0...@gmxpro.de> wrote:
> I'm playing nethack on a notebook (so there is no number pad) unter
> Linux. A while ago i played under Windows (and even longer ago under
> DOS). Those version could use the cursor / arrow keys to move. Linux cannot.

     NetHack expects keys to send single characters, and its
internal command handling it based on processing single characters.
Extra keys like arrows tend to send multi-character escape sequences,
and nethack will see each character as a separate command.  (MSDOS
could handle keyboard scan codes directly and convert them into
characters; the Windows port began life emulating that and has had
trouble with non-English keyboards because of it.  The VMS port
recognizes DEC VT-specific escape sequences using timed input to
try to gather characters sent at once, but it's not robust in a
network environment.  Fortunately no one's been foolish enough to
take the Unix/linux port down that path....)

     There's nothing you can do within nethack to alter what happens
when arrow keys are pressed.  Under X Windows you have full control,
and could make left-arrow send 'h' and so forth, but it's not for
the faint of heart.  Outside of X, I don't know how much control you
have.

> now I understand that the obvious solution is to learn the VI keys. But I
> always mistake 'j' and 'k' or 'h' and 'j' so I very often move in wrong
> ways.

     The obvious solution is also the best solution.  Show me a
keyboard which adds diagonal arrow keys and then you might be able
to persuade me that using arrows makes sense.  If you actually
use the vi-style keys, you will eventually become confortable with
them and not even have to think about which key does what (except
perhaps for y and z...).  It doesn't require special effort or
concentration, just ordinary practice and a modicum of patience.

> Is there a way to make the arrow keys usable? Some kind of key mapping
> or such a magic?
>
> The next problem I have is that I'm using a german keyboard where 'y'
> and 'z' are swapped against the US-keyboard, so one of the diagonal keys
> is not in the right place. Can I map those two keys vice versa (but only
> for nethack and only those two, otherwise I might use the us keyboard
> layout).

     There is a patch which does that, but it produces a hard-coded
change--everyone running that copy of the program will have those
keys swapped.  That'd be fine for local use but not work for a
network server.  The post-3.4.3 development code has a change to
support this remapping dynamically (just those two letters, not a
more general key mapping capability, at least so far...).  True,
that doesn't do you much good right now.  :-}

     You can find the compile-time patch in Pasi Kallinen's patch
database at http://bilious.homelinux.org/ under "qwertz layout
support".  You'll have to rebuild the program from sources in order
to make use of it.


